COMMAND NAME: gpdetective

Collects diagnostic information from a running Greenplum Database system.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpdetective [-h <master_hostname>] [-p <master_port>] [-U <gp_superuser>] 
[-P <password>] [--start_date <number_of_days> | <YYYY-MM-DD>] 
[--end_date | <YYYY-MM-DD>] [--diagnostics=a | n | s | o | c] 
[--logs=a | n | <dbid_list>] [--cores=t | f] [--pg_dumpall=t | f] 
[--pg_dump_options=<options>] [--tempdir=<temp_dir>] [--connect=t | f]

gpdetective -? 

gpdetective -v


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpdetective utility collects information from a running Greenplum 
Database system and creates a bzip2-compressed tar output file. This 
output file can then be sent to Greenplum Customer Support to help with 
the diagnosis of Greenplum Database errors or system failures. 
The gpdetective utility runs the following diagnostic tests:

* gpstate to check the system status

* gpcheckos to make sure the recommended OS settings are set on 
  all hosts

* pg_dumpall to capture the schema DDL for all databases

* gpcheckcat and gpcheckdb to check the system catalog tables for 
  inconsistencies

* pg_dump to capture a backup of the database in a single (non-parallel)
  dump file (you can configure this output by using pg_dump_options)

gpdetective captures the following information:

* The master and segment postgresql.conf configuration file

* The master and segment server log files (provided that the log files 
  are located in the default location)

* Core files (optional)

* Array configuration information

* Schema information

* A backup of the database

A bzip2-compressed tar output file containing this information is created 
in the current directory with a file name of gpdetective<timestamp>.tar.bz2.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-h <master_hostname>

The host name of the machine on which the Greenplum master database server 
is running. If not specified, reads from the environment variable PGHOST 
or defaults to localhost.


-p <master_port>

The TCP port on which the Greenplum master database server is 
listening for connections. If not specified, reads from the environment 
variable PGPORT or defaults to 5432.


-U <gp_superuser>

The Greenplum database superuser role name to connect as (typically 
gpadmin). If not specified, reads from the environment variable 
PGUSER or defaults to the current system user name.


-P <password>

If Greenplum Database is configured to use password authentication, you 
must also supply the database superuser password. If it is not specified, 
it reads from ~/.pgpass if it exists.


--start_date <number_of_days> | <YYYY-MM-DD>

Sets the start date for the diagnostic information collected. It can 
specify either the number of days prior, or an explicit past date.


--end_date | <YYYY-MM-DD>

Sets the end date for the diagnostic information collected. The 
collected information ends at 00:00:00 of the specified date. 


--diagnostics=a | n | s | o | c

Specifies the diagnostics that are run on the system. Enter either all 
(a), none (n), operating system (o) diagnostics, or catalog (c) 
diagnostics on the master catalog table. The default is all.


--logs=a | n | <dbid_list>

Specifies the segment log file(s) to retrieve. Enter "a" to get logs 
from all segments and mirrors, "n" to skip retrieval, or enter the 
database ID of the segment or master from which you want to get logs. 
You can also enter a range of IDs by entering a dash (-) between the 
IDs (for example, entering "3-6" retrieves logs from databases with 
the IDs 3, 4, 5, and 6). The default is [a]ll.


--cores=t | f 

Determines whether or not the utility retrieves core files. Enter "t" 
to get core files, or "f" to skip the retrieval of these files. The 
default is [t]rue.


--pg_dumpall=t | f

Specifies whether of not to run pg_dumpall. The pg_dumpall utility 
extracts all databases in a Greenplum Database system to a single script 
file or other archive file. Enter "t" to run pg_dumpall, or "f" to 
prevent this utility from running. The default is [t]rue.


--pg_dump_options=<options>

Specifies the pg_dump options to use in conjunction with the gpdetective 
utility. See pg_dump for a complete list of valid pg_dump 
options. 


--tempdir=<temp_dir>

Specifies the temporary directory that gpdetective uses store 
diagnostics information. The default value is determined by the 
$TEMP, $TMP and $TMPDIR environment variables.


--connect=t | f

Allows or denies gpdetective from making a database connection. If you 
enter "f" (false), gpdetective only gathers information it can obtain 
without making a connection to the database. The default is [t]rue.


-v (show utility version)

Displays the version of this utility.


-? (help)

Displays the utility usage and syntax.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Collect the diagnostic information for the past two days of activity
in a Greenplum Database system:

gpdetective --start_date=2

Collect diagnostic information for a Greenplum Database system:

gpdetective 

Collect diagnostic information for a Greenplum Database system 
and supply the required connection information for the master host:

gpdetective -h mdw -p 54320 - U gpadmin -P mypassword


